      "output": "-1",
      "explanation": "Generated from the verified optimal implementation."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(grid: number[][]): number`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Model each clear cell as a node and each pair of clear cells that are edge or corner adjacent as an edge of cost 1.",
    "With uniform edge cost, the shortest path is found by exploring in rings from the start.",
    "Push (0, 0) with distance 1, mark cells visited the moment they enter the queue, and stop the first time the bottom-right cell comes out."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force is Bellman-Ford style relaxation on the grid. It sets dist[0][0] = 1 and then sweeps every cell repeatedly, each time relaxing its distance to one more than the smallest distance among its eight clear neighbours, stopping when a full sweep changes nothing. Because a shortest path in a cell graph uses at most rows*cols cells, that many sweeps are enough to reach the fixed point, which costs O((rows*cols)^2) with an eight-way constant. The optimal version is a plain BFS: push (0, 0) with distance 1, expand through clear unvisited neighbours, and return the distance when the bottom-right cell leaves the queue.",
    "why_optimal": "Every move costs 1, so BFS discovers cells in non-decreasing distance order and the first time the target is dequeued its distance is optimal; each cell is enqueued at most once, giving O(rows*cols) time and O(rows*cols) space for the distance array and queue. The relaxation brute force re-examines all cells on every sweep, which is why it is a factor of rows*cols slower. O(rows*cols) is the floor here since both the start cell and the target cell must be examined and there are rows*cols cells to weigh, and at 20x20 the brute force still completes in a few hundred thousand operations.",
    "pitfalls": "Forgetting that the eight-neighbour move set makes the graph different from the 4-neighbour one: with diagonal moves a path can cut corners, so a 4-direction BFS returns too large a value. Length counts cells, not edges, so the distance array must start at 1 and the answer for a 1x1 grid is 1, not 0. A blocked start or a blocked end returns -1 without any search. Marking a cell visited when it is pushed rather than when it is popped is what keeps the BFS linear; deferring the mark lets the queue explode with duplicates."
  },
